WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.290
Inference optimal vision language models need only one visual
token, but larger models.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.100
From Carnegie Mellon and the Bosch Center for AI.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:20.778
A vision language model runs the image through an encoder like
CLIP, and hands the resulting patch tokens to a language

00:00:21.228 --> 00:00:28.620
For CLIP ViT-L at standard resolution, that is five hundred
and seventy-six tokens for a single image.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.766
The user's actual question is maybe fifty tokens.

00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:41.448
So the image accounts for over ninety percent of what the
language model processes, and therefore most of the latency.

00:00:42.798 --> 00:00:48.630
Inference cost is the standard estimate: parameters times
tokens processed.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:58.320
The tokens split three ways — the text prompt Q, the visual
tokens V that survive compression, and the generated tokens G.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:03.522
Because it is a product, there are two independent ways to
make a model cheaper.

00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:13.932
Shrink the language model, or send it fewer visual tokens.
Both cost you accuracy, and it is not obvious which you should

00:01:15.282 --> 00:01:22.098
Token compression is a small learnable module sitting between
the vision encoder and the language model.

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:34.212
The encoder produces n tokens laid out as a square grid of
image patches. The projector maps them to m tokens, with m

00:01:34.662 --> 00:01:44.526
One common design collapses each s-by-s region of patches into
a single token, then attends within that region to recover

00:01:44.976 --> 00:01:55.752
Worth being precise: this is not the same as using a smaller
encoder or a lower resolution image. Those barely reduce the

00:01:57.102 --> 00:02:00.006
Which brings the actual question into focus.

00:02:00.456 --> 00:02:09.336
Given a fixed inference budget, what is the right trade
between language model size and visual token count? Nobody had

00:02:10.686 --> 00:02:17.358
So they fit one. Downstream error as a joint function of
parameters and visual tokens.

00:02:17.808 --> 00:02:26.232
Alpha says how fast error falls as the language model grows. A
better pretrained family gives a larger alpha.

00:02:26.682 --> 00:02:34.722
Beta says how fast error falls as you add visual tokens back.
A better compression algorithm gives a larger beta.

00:02:35.172 --> 00:02:47.412
Fitted on Qwen-1.5 backbones from half a billion to seven
billion parameters, and token counts from one to five hundred

00:02:47.862 --> 00:02:59.190
Alpha is zero point zero seven seven. Beta is zero point zero
one five. Error moves about five times faster with parameters

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:09.660
Plotted against inference FLOPs, each curve is one language
model size and each dot along it is a different visual token

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:22.566
Two things stand out. First, error falls log-linearly as you
add visual tokens back — there is no free lunch, contrary to

00:03:23.016 --> 00:03:31.512
The authors attribute that discrepancy to narrow evaluation
suites that do not capture the model's full capability.

00:03:31.962 --> 00:03:37.530
Second, the curves are stacked by model size, and the gaps
between them are large.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:49.716
The dotted line is the Pareto frontier: the lowest error
achievable at each compute budget. It runs along the largest

00:03:51.066 --> 00:03:54.474
Fix the compute budget and read off the options.

00:03:54.924 --> 00:04:02.916
A half-billion model with all five hundred and seventy-six
tokens. A one-point-eight billion model with a hundred and

00:04:03.366 --> 00:04:08.286
Four billion with sixty-four. Seven billion with thirty-six.

00:04:08.736 --> 00:04:15.936
Each step trades visual tokens for parameters at constant
cost, and each step lowers the error.

00:04:16.386 --> 00:04:24.834
Push it all the way and the compute-optimal configuration is
the largest model you can afford, fed a single visual token.

00:04:26.184 --> 00:04:29.904
That is the headline, and it is genuinely surprising.

00:04:30.354 --> 00:04:38.562
For visual reasoning, one token plus a big model beats many
tokens plus a small one, at equal cost.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:46.560
But the single-token result assumes the text prompt is cached,
so Q is effectively zero.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:54.642
That is realistic for a fixed prompt — a driver assistance
system asking the same question of every frame.

00:04:55.092 --> 00:05:04.428
When the prompt varies, the model pays a fixed cost for the
text no matter what, and a few extra visual tokens become

00:05:04.878 --> 00:05:12.918
At fifty text tokens the optimum moves to around sixteen
visual tokens. At a hundred it climbs further.

00:05:13.368 --> 00:05:22.608
So the honest version of the claim is: as few visual tokens as
the prompt length allows, and the biggest model that fits.

00:05:23.958 --> 00:05:25.782
Two checks are worth noting.

00:05:26.232 --> 00:05:37.488
The laws were fitted only up to seven billion parameters, then
used to predict a fourteen billion model. They land within two

00:05:37.938 --> 00:05:47.802
And a negative result the authors report honestly: repeating
the compressed visual tokens to burn more compute produced no

00:05:48.252 --> 00:06:00.516
That is a real contrast with text language models, where extra
test-time compute helps. Their reading is that current VLM

00:06:01.866 --> 00:06:06.066
Now the part that stops this from being a universal rule.

00:06:06.516 --> 00:06:13.932
Refit the same law on DocVQA and TextVQA, where the task is
reading text out of an image.

00:06:14.382 --> 00:06:22.734
Alpha drops to zero point zero two nine and beta rises to zero
point zero four eight. The ordering inverts.

00:06:23.184 --> 00:06:33.744
For document tasks, visual tokens dominate and extra
parameters barely help. A page of dense text simply does not

00:06:34.194 --> 00:06:39.162
Same architecture, same scaling law, opposite prescription.

00:06:40.512 --> 00:06:45.480
Here is the split on real benchmarks at matched inference
cost.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:57.570
Swapping a small model with many tokens for a large model with
few gives large gains on the reasoning suites — over thirty

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:04.236
POPE is roughly flat, which is a hallucination benchmark
rather than a reasoning one.

00:07:04.686 --> 00:07:11.670
And on DocVQA and TextVQA the same trade loses you double-
digit percentages.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:22.104
The lesson is not that one token is always right. It is that
the right answer depends on whether your task needs to read

00:07:23.454 --> 00:07:26.622
Which leads to the paper's argument about the field.

00:07:27.072 --> 00:07:40.080
Most token compression work targets a modest reduction — five
hundred and seventy-six down to a hundred and forty-four, or

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:50.610
But the compute-optimal regime is one, four or sixteen tokens.
That is ninety-seven to ninety-nine point eight percent

00:07:51.060 --> 00:08:02.076
An algorithm tuned to lose nothing at four-times compression
is not obviously the right algorithm at five-hundred-times

00:08:03.426 --> 00:08:08.250
So they propose a compressor designed for that regime, called
QueCC.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:17.700
The idea is simple. When you only get one token, which part of
the image you keep matters enormously — and that depends on

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:31.926
So the user's query is injected before compression rather than
after. The last hidden state of the query, taken from the

00:08:32.376 --> 00:08:43.872
Those query-fused tokens are downsampled by a learnable depth-
wise convolution, then local cross-attention runs between each

00:08:44.322 --> 00:08:55.818
An MLP maps the result into the language model's input space.
And when the prompt is fixed, that query embedding can be

00:08:57.168 --> 00:09:02.136
At one visual token, QueCC edges out the alternatives.

00:09:02.586 --> 00:09:13.410
Measured as the gap to the uncompressed LLaVA baseline, it
closes twelve percent of that gap on MME and nineteen percent

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.276
Note the last bar though. Uncompressed still scores far
higher.

00:09:18.726 --> 00:09:31.158
The claim was never that one token matches five hundred and
seventy-six. It is that one token with a bigger model beats

00:09:32.508 --> 00:09:45.084
To summarise: for visual reasoning, parameters buy roughly
five times more accuracy per FLOP than visual tokens do, so

00:09:45.534 --> 00:09:48.966
But the caveats matter here more than usual.

00:09:49.416 --> 00:10:00.336
The law is fitted on one model family, one compressor and one
encoder, so alpha describes Qwen-1.5, not language models in

00:10:00.786 --> 00:10:10.962
The vision encoder's cost is excluded as a constant —
reasonable at five hundred tokens, much less so at one, where

00:10:11.412 --> 00:10:19.188
The single-token result needs a cacheable prompt. With a real
varying query the optimum is nearer sixteen.

00:10:19.638 --> 00:10:25.950
And for anything involving reading text off an image, the
entire recommendation inverts.

